<title>NETHERLANDS: FORECAST - Dutch see 1998 GDP growth of 3.25 pct.</title>
<headline>FORECAST - Dutch see 1998 GDP growth of 3.25 pct.</headline>
<dateline>THE HAGUE 1997-04-03</dateline>
<text>
<p>The Dutch government's Central Planning Bureau (CPB) published the following forecasts for the Dutch economy on Thursday:</p>
<p>						   1998   1997   1996   1995</p>
<p>    Gross domestic product		 3.25   3.0    2.8    2.1</p>
<p>    Private consumption		    2.25   2.25   3.1    2.1</p>
<p>    Gross business investment	    2.75   4.75   7.8   10.1</p>
<p>    Business production		    3.5    3.25   3.0    2.2</p>
<p>    Exports				    7.25   5.75   4.1    8.6</p>
<p>    Imports				    5.5    5.5    4.7    8.3</p>
<p>    Contract wages			   3.0    2.5    1.6    1.4</p>
<p>    Consumer price index		   2.0    2.75   2.1    2.0</p>
<p>    Current account surplus		6.0    5.25   5.1    5.1</p>
<p>    Unemployment (thousands)	     420    465    494    533</p>
<p>    Unemployment (pct labour force)    5.0    5.75   6.3    6.5</p>
<p>    Dollar rate in guilders		1.80   1.80   1.69   1.61</p>
<p>    10-yr Dutch govt bond yield (pct)  5.75   5.75   6.2    6.9</p>
<p>    EMU deficit				1.7    2.3    2.4    4.0</p>
<p>    EMU debt				   71.9   73.8   78.0   79.7</p>
<p>NOTES - All figures show real percentage changes versus previous year unless otherwise stated in table or below. The CBS rounds off its estimates to nearest 0.25 percent.</p>
<p>Gross business investment excludes housing. Exports exclude energy. Current account surplus is expressed as percentage of GDP. Unemployment is stated according to domestic definition in thousands of people and according to standardised international definition as percentage of labour force.</p>
<p>-- Andrew Kelly, Amsterdam newsroom +31 20 504 5000</p>
</text>
